From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: macbox <macbox@localhost>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 05:28:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/33] staged page-ups: a new texture's first page-up copies
 its guest pages into a runtime buffer when no live view covers them

A new paged texture's first page-up went through a view: one mmap per run of guest pages
(~600 for a 2.4 MB texture of the Sim's compositor), a Vulkan import and, at its release, a
munmap that flushes the TLB once per run on every CPU the process ran on, the vCPUs too.
Measured with one Sim: ~520 views/s, all first page-ups of new Shared render targets, 15%
of the Sim task thread, ~57k TLB shootdowns/s.

memory::page_refs counts the live views over each guest page (new_range_mapping adds,
Mapping's last release removes). The GPU reaches guest RAM only through such views, each
alive until the runtime frees its last buffer, after the GPU's last use; so pages no live
view covers have no GPU access pending, and a CPU copy at the exec reads the bytes the
view's blit would read. Covered pages take the view as ever.

Switch: REIMS_VGPU_STAGED_PAGE_UP=<path> counts from the start and stages while the file
exists (looked at once a second). Unset: nothing counted, nothing changed.
